Z||000000||Joe Trainer||05-13-2000||07:35 PM||||I've looked through the manuals and must be overlooking something, but can someone tell me the PSI reading on the high side of a full charged R-12 system? Thanks in advance.  Joe||||reg||5
Z||000001||Ed||05-14-2000||12:42 AM||||I've got the shop manual with all the numbers you'd want.  <P>However, they're all through the diagnosis charts and I don't which is which!   <P>Does 50 psi sound about right?||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||05-14-2000||11:03 AM||||You need to find a chart. system pressure depends on both what refrigerant, and the temperature.<P>No ed, not even close.<P>I can't rememeber the numbers but that would be 2 low even for a good low side.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||05-14-2000||01:28 PM||||Remember that if the compressor is not running the high and low side will be the same. Seem to remember 50 as being around right for a system that is not running. Get a chart -as was mentioned a lot depends on ambient temp.<p>[This message has been edited by Phil (edited 05-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Joe Trainer||05-15-2000||12:11 AM||||Looked through the service manual again and the A/C performance test chart jumped out at me.  The hi side pressures run from 150 to 330 psi depending on ambient temp. and humidity.  Engine running at 2000 rpm.  Thanks for the replies, they made me look again and again at the service manual.  Joe||||reg||1
